Assume fork() never fails and that everything except sleep() takes negligible time.

```c
int main(void) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        if (fork() == 0) {
            sleep(i + 1);     // child i exits at (its start time) + i + 1
            exit(0);
        }
        if (i == 1)
            wait(NULL);
    }
    sleep(10);
    return 0;
}
```

How many **zombie** processes created by this program exist at t = 6 s?

Assume orphans are adopted by init (PID 1), which promptly reaps its terminated children, no subreaper exists, and only sleep() takes significant time (fork, exit and waitpid are instantaneous). Times are measured from the start of `main`.

```c
int main(void) {                 // process P
    pid_t a = fork();
    if (a == 0) {                // child A
        if (fork() == 0) {       // grandchild B (child of A)
            sleep(2);
            exit(0);
        }
        sleep(4);
        exit(0);                 // A never calls wait()
    }
    if (fork() == 0) {           // child C
        sleep(1);
        exit(0);
    }
    sleep(3);
    waitpid(a, NULL, 0);
    sleep(10);
    return 0;
}
```

